---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import quotes from "../data/quotes.json";

const quoteCount = quotes.length.toLocaleString("en-US");
const thinkerCount = new Set(quotes.map((quote) => quote.author)).size;

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "AboutPage",
  name: "About Philosophy Blind Box",
  description:
    "Philosophy Blind Box is a curated archive of English philosophy quotations, presented at random.",
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About | Philosophy Blind Box"
  description="Philosophy Blind Box is a curated archive of English philosophy quotations from East and West, presented at random."
  canonical="/about"
  schema={schema}
>
  <div class="page-shell">
    <header class="page-intro">
      <h1>An archive of chance encounters</h1>
      <p>
        Philosophy Blind Box gathers verified English quotations and presents
        one at random, so reading can begin before selection.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose-layout">
      <aside class="prose-aside">
        <p>
          {quoteCount} passages from {thinkerCount} thinkers across Greco-Roman,
          European, American, Chinese, Indian, Buddhist, Jewish, Islamic, and
          Japanese traditions.
        </p>
      </aside>
      <article class="prose">
        <h2>Why random?</h2>
        <p>
          Choosing a theme too quickly can narrow attention before the text is
          heard. Chance reverses that order. A passage arrives first; judgment
          and context follow.
        </p>

        <h2>English for every reader</h2>
        <p>
          This site is built for English readers. Each quotation appears in a
          carefully checked English rendering—never in Greek, Latin, Sanskrit,
          or other source languages as the primary text. Source notes remain for
          context, but the encounter itself stays in English.
        </p>

        <h2>Editorial method</h2>
        <p>
          The corpus is a verified selection: attributions checked, wording
          aligned with reliable sources, and passages chosen for clarity and
          lasting insight. Political slogans, opaque jargon, and misattributed
          sayings are excluded. Each entry records thinker, school, source, and
          theme.
        </p>

        <h2>Browse beyond chance</h2>
        <p>
          After a random encounter, continue by thinker or theme. The random
          view is an entrance, not a limit.
        </p>

        <h2>A closing note</h2>
        <p>
          Philosophy does not answer. It hands you a mirror. This site simply
          keeps a place where that mirror may appear, one unexpected sentence at
          a time.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
